WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:02.760
"Is there an increase in taxes on the

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:06.220
middle class to pay for Medicare For All,

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:06.935
 

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.660
or rather, where would the tax burden go to pay for that?"
[Sanders] "Good, OK."

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.560
A little over four very,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.075
very long years ago, I made a video about

00:01:33.075 --> 00:01:36.460
the way then-candidate Donald Trump answers a question.

00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:40.045
I tried to show that Trump's salesman-like phraseology was

00:01:40.135 --> 00:01:43.155
very effective at communicating a feeling to the listener,

00:01:43.155 --> 00:01:45.865
even if the actual claims were bogus or

00:01:45.865 --> 00:01:48.765
nonsensical. I don't think this is the only reason he won

00:01:48.765 --> 00:01:51.795
in 2016, but his skill as a communicator was definitely

00:01:51.795 --> 00:01:54.455
a big part. Like Trump, Bernie

00:01:54.455 --> 00:01:57.315
Sanders, who is currently leading the contest for the democratic

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.780
nomination for president, has a talent

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.340
for communication. In a million ways that I won't

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.900
enumerate here, these two men are very

00:02:05.905 --> 00:02:08.925
different, obviously. But the polls and the

00:02:08.925 --> 00:02:11.805
votes show that they both know how to connect

00:02:11.805 --> 00:02:14.845
with voters. And a significant reason for this, I think,

00:02:14.845 --> 00:02:17.355
is the way they wield language.

00:02:18.395 --> 00:02:21.445
Today, I want to take a look at Sanders' answer to a question that

00:02:21.445 --> 00:02:23.965
he gets a lot:
[Colbert] "Is there an increase in taxes

00:02:23.965 --> 00:02:26.855
on the middle class to pay for

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:29.695
Medicare For All?"
And I think an interesting place to

00:02:29.695 --> 00:02:32.675
start might be to look back at what I said about Trump's

00:02:32.675 --> 00:02:35.805
speaking style and see how Sanders compares.

00:02:35.805 --> 00:02:38.595
After all, both these answers happen to be the

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:42.395
same number of words: 220. Though Sanders

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:45.315
takes an extra 30 seconds to get through them, which

00:02:45.315 --> 00:02:48.335
I'll get back to in a little bit. So these are the four things

00:02:48.335 --> 00:02:50.875
I said about Trump's speech. The first two

00:02:50.875 --> 00:02:53.785
are about his simplicity, both in the words

00:02:53.785 --> 00:02:55.160
he chooses:

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.500
And the way he constructs sentences:

00:03:02.185 --> 00:03:05.275
The third thing I noted was his ability to re-frame an

00:03:05.275 --> 00:03:08.215
assumed negative reaction to what he was saying by

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.960
his use of the second-person point-of-view:

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:13.920
And by implicating

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.760
us in what he's saying, as if we've

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.720
already agreed:

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:23.020
And finally, I pointed out how Trump likes to end

00:03:23.025 --> 00:03:25.935
his sentences with punch, with words

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.025
that will leave you with a thematic sense of the emotional

00:03:29.025 --> 00:03:30.780
content he's trying to convey:

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.820
 

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.540
So how does Bernie compare?

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.535
What about his word choice? Trump favors short

00:03:40.535 --> 00:03:43.865
words, about three-quarters of his answer were one syllable.

00:03:43.995 --> 00:03:46.975
Well, it was the same for Bernie: 74%.

00:03:46.975 --> 00:03:49.885
Here's how he stacks up against Trump with more complicated words.

00:03:49.885 --> 00:03:52.785
Looks like Bernie uses significantly more

00:03:52.785 --> 00:03:55.545
three-syllable words, but otherwise a somewhat

00:03:55.545 --> 00:03:58.415
similar breakdown. They both understand that

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:01.315
rhythmic series of short, simple words can

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.220
burrow into the listener's head:

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:05.460
 

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.155
Of course, Bernie doesn't need to use short

00:04:08.155 --> 00:04:10.755
words to find rhythm. The reason he

00:04:10.755 --> 00:04:13.745
takes longer than Trump to get through the same amount of words is because

00:04:13.745 --> 00:04:16.495
he gives every single syllable

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.080
space to breathe:

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.180
Now in sentence

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.865
construction, Bernie's answer is more complex than

00:04:24.865 --> 00:04:27.885
Trump's. He uses more compound sentences. In fact,

00:04:27.885 --> 00:04:30.800
he starts his answer with two dependent clauses,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.340
back-to-back.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.580
 

00:04:35.585 --> 00:04:38.505
He's also talking a lot about things that require

00:04:38.505 --> 00:04:40.915
a basic understanding of politics,

00:04:40.915 --> 00:04:43.865
health care, and taxation, which makes his language

00:04:43.865 --> 00:04:46.815
necessarily more sophisticated. As for

00:04:46.815 --> 00:04:49.395
reframing negative reaction, he does that

00:04:49.395 --> 00:04:52.265
in a similar way to Trump, by putting the listener

00:04:52.265 --> 00:04:55.285
directly into his answer, in this case using Colbert

00:04:55.285 --> 00:04:57.435
as a stand-in for all of us.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.460
 

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.460
And finally,

00:05:03.465 --> 00:05:05.800
yes, he does end his sentences with punch,

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.945
just not in the way that Trump does by using thematic words.

00:05:08.945 --> 00:05:11.245
Sanders uses epistrophe,

00:05:11.245 --> 00:05:13.940
the stylistic device in which you repeat words at the end of

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.300
sentences for effect.

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:19.835
"Just talked to a woman the other day who paid $1,700 a month, $20,000 a year - gone.

00:05:19.835 --> 00:05:22.825
No more copayments - gone.

00:05:22.825 --> 00:05:25.520
No more out of pocket expenses - gone.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.020
...All of that is gone...That's gone.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.415
You can almost see your out-of-pocket medical bills blipping

00:05:31.415 --> 00:05:33.975
out of existence. So there are

00:05:34.285 --> 00:05:37.215
actually some interesting similarities in the way Trump

00:05:37.215 --> 00:05:38.375
and Bernie speak.

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:42.075
But of course, these answers aren't perfectly comparable.

00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:43.075
And there are

00:05:43.285 --> 00:05:46.315
a lot of differences in their styles as well.

00:05:46.315 --> 00:05:49.555
Trump is singular in the way he improvs stream-of-consciousness

00:05:49.555 --> 00:05:52.605
rants that often look incoherent when read

00:05:52.605 --> 00:05:55.935
back, yet connect with his supporters in the moment.

00:05:56.085 --> 00:05:59.455
Sanders, on the other hand, has been preaching about the same things

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:02.245
for decades. He's sharpened his message

00:06:02.245 --> 00:06:05.715
to a fine point,
which is extra impressive when you consider

00:06:05.845 --> 00:06:08.875
that so much of his rhetoric has to do with context.

00:06:08.875 --> 00:06:11.575
"Is there an increase in taxes

00:06:11.575 --> 00:06:14.275
on the middle class to pay for

00:06:14.275 --> 00:06:15.275
Medicare For All,

00:06:15.685 --> 00:06:18.875
or rather, where would the tax burden go to pay for that?"

00:06:18.875 --> 00:06:21.655
Colbert asks him if he's going to raise taxes, and to his credit,

00:06:21.655 --> 00:06:25.085
he's willing to answer yes, but it's here,

00:06:25.525 --> 00:06:28.235
couched in 200 words of fine-tuned

00:06:28.235 --> 00:06:31.525
context. He has to explain why, in his view,

00:06:31.525 --> 00:06:34.785
raising taxes isn't the scary thing it's assumed to be,

00:06:34.785 --> 00:06:37.855
when you factor in all your premiums and copayments,

00:06:37.855 --> 00:06:40.675
the artificially high prices of prescriptions drugs

00:06:40.675 --> 00:06:43.875
set by a pharmaceutical industry that's ripping us off,

00:06:44.035 --> 00:06:47.345
he's ensuring us that the richer will get taxed more,

00:06:47.345 --> 00:06:50.235
and struggling people not at all.
Overall,

00:06:50.265 --> 00:06:53.525
we'll pay less in taxes, he wants to say, since current

00:06:53.525 --> 00:06:56.365
health care costs are a tax in all but

00:06:56.365 --> 00:06:59.175
name, one that can be and has been

00:06:59.175 --> 00:07:02.365
devastating to many among us.
For Bernie, I've noticed

00:07:02.435 --> 00:07:05.075
that foregrounding the important context

00:07:05.075 --> 00:07:07.565
is a game of volume—volume

00:07:07.565 --> 00:07:10.735
and emphasis. While it might seem like he's yelling all

00:07:10.735 --> 00:07:13.655
the time, Bernie does modulate a lot, and if you

00:07:13.655 --> 00:07:16.445
pick out the moments when his pitch or his volume

00:07:16.445 --> 00:07:18.920
ramps up, you can see a sketch

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.480
of what he wants the listener to take away.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.715
"Nobody in America will pay any more premiums...

00:07:24.715 --> 00:07:25.400
No more copayments...

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:27.500
No more out of pocket expenses...

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:29.400
Nobody will go bankrupt...

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.100
All of that is gone...

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.400
For their prescription drugs...

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:34.400
In a progressive way...

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.900
If I say to you, you're $10,000 to the good,

00:07:37.925 --> 00:07:39.995
You'd ask me, where do I sign on for that."

00:07:40.335 --> 00:07:42.445
As with my Trump video four years ago,

00:07:42.445 --> 00:07:45.525
I'm not trying to make a comment on Bernie Sanders'

00:07:45.525 --> 00:07:48.305
politics. I'm just obsessed with

00:07:48.305 --> 00:07:51.615
the way politicians attempt to communicate with the voters.

00:07:51.615 --> 00:07:54.635
Sanders obviously has a good sense of rhythm

00:07:54.635 --> 00:07:57.935
and delivery and an iron grip on the points he wants to make.

00:07:57.935 --> 00:08:00.805
But what's even more masterful is his ability to

00:08:00.805 --> 00:08:03.875
articulate an ideology out of all the things that surround

00:08:03.875 --> 00:08:06.905
his answer to a question in a way that

00:08:06.905 --> 00:08:10.025
often makes the questions feel not only stupid,

00:08:10.025 --> 00:08:13.025
but a symptom of the stupid worldview that he's

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:14.275
trying to fight against.

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:17.845
Now, it remains to be seen whether those principles can win the

00:08:17.845 --> 00:08:20.720
presidency or even the democratic nomination.

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.680
Regardless, they have a powerful messenger.

